// `n` is an unannotated closure parameter, so its type is inferred.
// The CONDITION `n == 0` pins n to Int; the then-branch `!n` needs
// Bool. `n` cannot be both, so this must be REJECTED.
//
// This fixture exists because the self-hosted checker accepted it.
// `TyEnv` records a binding with whatever type it had when bound and
// nothing rewrites the environment afterwards, so reading `n` back
// inside the branch returned its original, unconstrained type
// variable. `!` unified that with Bool locally, and `subst_compose`
// keeps the already-established binding for a repeated variable — so
// the conflicting one was silently discarded rather than reported.
//
// `check` answered "ok" and codegen went on to emit `xor i1` against
// an `i64`; the only thing that ever complained was `clang`, with no
// source location. Fixed by resolving identifiers against the
// threaded substitution at lookup (see `infer_ident`).
let main (): Unit = {
    let f = |n| if n == 0 { !n } else { true };
    println(f(1));
}
